{
  "title": "Trapped",
  "tagline": "A photographic study of what it means to be a woman in a modern society.",
  "memo": [
    "I was born in a society where women had to fight for every single right that they wanted to have. I grew up in a Muslim country and learned to be a Muslim woman and practice the religion by force rather than choice. I always struggled and questioned why I shouldn't be given the same rights and freedom as the opposite sex. Every day I questioned if I am smart enough for math and engineering. All the time I had to convince everyone around me that I am capable of doing anything and I am not limited just because I am a woman. I fought my way to get admitted to the best engineering school in the world, MIT, however I still heard people mumble I only got in because I am a girl.",
    "After three years at MIT, in my junior year I found myself in a place where I was sad, lonely, hopeless, had strong doubt about my abilities, and I felt Trapped by everything that was out of my control; every limitation that I faced because of everything that I had no power over — my gender, my race, my country. It was then when I started to share my feelings via photography. The more I captured, and shared my work, the more I realized the power of photography. I started capturing my feeling of being “Trapped” by asking some of my close friends to model for me. After the first few photoshoots I started asking around for more models. I talked to each of them to learn about their background and the struggles they faced in their life because of their gender; then I would design a scene to capture their story. It was then that I discovered how all women have to fight for rights that should already be there. This project started as a self projection, however the more the project progressed the more I saw its impact, not only on the models but also on the people observing the outcomes. Each photo portrays a story, a struggle, a woman. This project, in its current form, is aimed to raise awareness about the struggles of being a woman in a modern society; it is aimed at an audience all around the world who speak any language, as the message is conveyed through the lens of a camera."
